我喜欢他们没有经历痛苦的离婚。

00:39:22.199 --> 00:39:23.039
I liked that.
我喜欢这点。

00:39:23.039 --> 00:39:27.880
I thought that was nice to see a couple that was like, they were through whatever the hard part was and they were running for each other.
我觉得看到一对这样的夫妻挺好的，他们已经熬过了最艰难的部分，而且他们是奔向彼此的。

00:39:27.880 --> 00:39:29.559
Ted's dad was like, he's a nice guy.
泰德的爸爸就像是在说，他是个好人。

00:39:29.559 --> 00:39:31.719
I love that it was Ted's worth a dentist or dentist.
我喜欢泰德的价值是一个牙医还是牙医。

00:39:31.719 --> 00:39:32.559
You remember him?
你还记得他吗？

00:39:32.559 --> 00:39:34.119
He did your braces.
他给你整过牙。

00:39:34.119 --> 00:39:38.280
And I love that it wasn't the cliche they fucking hate each other reveal.
而且我喜欢这并没有落入“他们恨死对方了”的俗套揭晓。

00:39:38.280 --> 00:39:40.159
The reveal was they're okay.
揭晓的结果是他们挺好的。

00:39:40.159 --> 00:39:42.039
They've processed it and they're okay.
他们已经接受并消化了，他们挺好的。

00:39:42.039 --> 00:39:43.800
This was the best thing for both of them.
这对他们俩来说都是最好的事。

00:39:43.800 --> 00:39:47.480
They just didn't bother to tell their children because it didn't feel Christmassy.
他们只是懒得告诉孩子们，因为这感觉不太适合圣诞节。

00:39:47.480 --> 00:39:48.639
Yeah.
对。

00:39:48.639 --> 00:39:58.230
You know, there was an episode of Curb Your Enthusiasm where Larry's mom died and they didn't tell him because they didn't want to bother him.
你知道，《抑制热情》里有一集，拉里的妈妈去世了，他们没告诉他，因为他们不想打扰他。

00:39:58.230 --> 00:39:59.550
I only heard about this one recently.
我最近才听说这个。

00:39:59.550 --> 00:40:00.789
I missed this one somehow.
我不知怎么漏掉了这一集。

00:40:00.789 --> 00:40:02.550
We did this before that, I think.
我想我们在这之前就做过了。

00:40:02.550 --> 00:40:05.389
I'm not saying Larry David ripped this off, but yeah.
我不是说拉里·大卫抄袭了这个，但是吧。

00:40:05.389 --> 00:40:15.369
No, we definitely, I don't think Larry David to watch the show, but that ending bit, that tag where Ted slowly realizes his grandmother's dad.
不，我们绝对……我不觉得拉里·大卫看了这部剧，但是那个结尾的片段，泰德慢慢意识到他奶奶的爸爸。

00:40:15.369 --> 00:40:16.530
It's no longer with us.
已经不在了。

00:40:16.530 --> 00:40:20.250
It was, you played that really, that was a great slow burn.
那是……你演得真的很好，那是一个非常棒的渐进式包袱。

00:40:20.250 --> 00:40:22.130
That was probably the biggest lap.
那可能全场笑得最大声的地方。

00:40:22.130 --> 00:40:25.809
Watching him, it's my wife, Rebecca was, that was her biggest laugh.
看着他，我妻子丽贝卡，那是她笑得最大声的地方。

00:40:25.809 --> 00:40:31.409
And another, by the way, I said, I think there's another dark family-related death joke, and this is it.
还有另外一个，顺便说一句，我说过，我觉得还有一个关于家庭死亡的黑暗笑话，就是这个。

00:40:31.409 --> 00:40:34.650
This is another Stephen Lloyd joke of the day grandma.
这是史蒂芬·劳埃德的又一个关于奶奶的笑话。

00:40:34.650 --> 00:40:37.170
That's a very, that's a very similar joke.
那是一个非常、非常相似的笑话。

00:40:37.170 --> 00:40:38.650
I said that with love and respect.
我是带着爱和敬意说这话的。

00:40:38.650 --> 00:40:41.650
Stephen Lloyd couldn't have too many dead grandparents.
史蒂芬·劳埃德的剧里死去的祖父母可不能太多。

00:40:41.650 --> 00:40:45.250
That was just, there's a body count in a Stephen Lloyd episode.
那简直是……史蒂芬·劳埃德的一集里可是有死亡人数统计的。

00:40:45.250 --> 00:40:45.750
Yeah.
对。

00:40:45.750 --> 00:40:47.548
It's like Quentin Tarantino.
就像昆汀·塔伦蒂诺一样。

00:40:47.369 --> 00:40:50.929
I liked when Barney said, oh, he mentioned he was divorced us in a totally space.
我喜欢巴尼说，哦，他在一个完全不相关的空间里提到他离婚了。

00:40:53.329 --> 00:40:56.570
Yes, I love that throw again, very well thrown away.
对，我喜欢那种漫不经心的小包袱，抛得太好了。

00:40:56.730 --> 00:40:58.610
So much of the best comedy is thrown away.
很多最好的喜剧都是漫不经心抛出来的。

00:40:58.610 --> 00:41:00.050
We were saying that about Michael Gross.
我们之前聊迈克尔·格罗斯时也这么说过。

00:41:00.050 --> 00:41:01.809
That's how he was on family ties.
他在《家庭纽带》里就是这样的。

00:41:01.809 --> 00:41:03.369
He was, he played this like that.
他当时就是这么演的。

00:41:03.369 --> 00:41:05.070
and that's a great throwaway joke.
那真是一个极好的随性笑话。

00:41:05.070 --> 00:41:09.070
That's if there's one lesson here today, just throw the jokes away.
如果今天能学到一个教训的话，那就是随性地把笑话抛出来就行。

00:41:09.070 --> 00:41:10.030
Don't hit him too hard.
别太用力。

00:41:10.030 --> 00:41:11.829
Even Ted going, sure.
甚至连泰德说“当然”。

00:41:11.829 --> 00:41:14.190
Like your little throwaway, sure, was my favorite joke in the last time.
就像你随口说的那句“当然”，是我上次最喜欢的笑话。

00:41:14.190 --> 00:41:19.030
I kind of think of those jokes a little bit, like a moth lance on your shoulder and you just go.
我有点把那些笑话想象成，就像一只蛾子落在你的肩膀上，然后你只是……

00:41:19.030 --> 00:41:20.349
Yeah, don't freak out.
对，别大惊小怪的。

00:41:20.349 --> 00:41:21.869
You just flick it away, you know?
你只是把它弹开，对吧？

00:41:21.869 --> 00:41:23.309
You just, it's just a little kick.
你只是，就轻轻掸一下。

00:41:23.309 --> 00:41:24.789
It just doesn't take me.
它不会影响我。

00:41:24.789 --> 00:41:26.190
That'll get it done.
这样就搞定了。

00:41:26.190 --> 00:41:33.150
I did find myself the scene outside with the four of them, Ted's parents and Robin and Ted.
我确实觉得外面那场戏很有意思，他们四个在一起，泰德的父母、罗宾和泰德。

00:41:33.150 --> 00:41:40.829
I thought it was so moving that just, when I have kids and I tell them the story of how I met their mother, I'm gonna tell them the whole damn story.
我觉得那真的很感人，就是，当我以后有了孩子，我要把我是怎么遇到他们母亲的故事讲给他们听时，我要把整个该死的故事都讲给他们听。

00:41:40.829 --> 00:41:41.670
Yeah.
对啊。

00:41:41.670 --> 00:41:48.929
He commits to, he's like, I don't like these, I don't like these half details.
他下定决心，他心想，我不喜欢这些，我不喜欢这种语焉不详的细节。

00:41:48.929 --> 00:41:55.170
I want to know these people more, I want to know where I came from, I want to know my history.
我想更多地了解这些人，我想知道我来自哪里，我想了解我的历史。

00:41:55.170 --> 00:42:01.409
And in some ways, he over-corrects, like he tells them too much, it could be argued.
在某种程度上，他矫枉过正了，比如说他告诉了他们太多，这可以说是肯定的。

00:42:01.409 --> 00:42:09.389
But there's something, I think every generation is trying to remedy something that they felt like they didn't get.
但有些东西，我觉得每一代人都在试图弥补某种他们觉得自己没有得到的东西。

00:42:09.389 --> 00:42:14.230
I want to explain myself to my kids because my parents didn't explain themselves to me.
我想向我的孩子们解释我自己，因为我的父母没有向我解释过他们自己。

00:42:14.230 --> 00:42:15.909
Yeah, what was it?
对啊，那是怎么回事？

00:42:15.909 --> 00:42:16.949
Oh, it's a great story.
哦，那是个很棒的故事。

00:42:16.949 --> 00:42:18.110
We met at a bar.
我们在酒吧相遇。

00:42:18.110 --> 00:42:23.670
Yeah, and this fellow visits an Irish bar, which is another Ted Robin parallel of how they met.
对，然后这家伙去了一家爱尔兰酒吧，这也是泰德和罗宾相遇方式的又一个平行呼应。

00:42:25.349 --> 00:42:29.429
There's so many smart meta kind of in jokes in this thing, Craig.
这里面有那么多聪明的元梗、藏梗，克雷格。

00:42:29.429 --> 00:42:30.269
It's really is later.
确实是这样，而且更深层。

00:42:30.269 --> 00:42:38.691
I know the internet folks tend to get a little conspiratorial, but they're like that because there is so much woven and it's densely packed with stuff.
我知道互联网上的朋友们往往有点阴谋论倾向，但他们之所以这样，是因为里面编织的东西实在是太多了，而且内容极其密集。

00:42:38.691 --> 00:42:46.110
It's Barney entering and thinking he's getting applause from what is clearly a wall if we're to believe the reality of the apartment.
比如巴尼走进来，以为自己赢得了一面墙的掌声，如果我们相信这间公寓的现实设定的。

00:42:46.110 --> 00:42:49.072
And then Ted is saying, I'm going to tell this show.
然后泰德说，我要讲述这个节目。

00:42:49.072 --> 00:42:51.692
This show you're seeing, I'm going to tell it.
你现在看到的这个节目，我要把它讲出来。

00:42:51.692 --> 00:42:52.262
It's 2006.
现在是2006年。

00:42:52.262 --> 00:42:57.275
Now I'm going to tell my kids in 2030 what's happening as we're all watching this story.
现在我要在2030年把正在发生的事情讲给我的孩子们听，就好像我们所有人都在看着这个故事一样。

00:42:57.275 --> 00:42:58.414
That is, I loved it.
那真是，我太喜欢了。

00:42:58.414 --> 00:43:08.725
That was my favorite moment of the whole episode, because that was the show explaining itself as a show, which is a story to kids and parents all want their kids to understand them.
那是整集里我最喜欢的时刻，因为那是剧集在以剧集本身的形式进行自我诠释，这是一个讲给孩子听的故事，而父母都希望他们的孩子能理解他们。

00:43:08.725 --> 00:43:12.030
And there's just something, there's a lot of layers there.
而且这里面有某种，有很多层次的东西。

00:43:12.030 --> 00:43:13.531
And I think it's very human.
而且我认为这非常人性化。

00:43:13.531 --> 00:43:14.604
We all tell stories.
我们都在讲故事。

00:43:14.604 --> 00:43:16.909
We all need to make sense of it in a story.
我们都需要在故事中把这一切理出头绪。

00:43:16.909 --> 00:43:21.673
And then you think, why is he taking this much time to tell in such granular detail this story?
然后你会想，他为什么花这么长时间，用如此细致的细节来讲述这个故事？

00:43:21.673 --> 00:43:24.030
And it's like, well, because he has this wound.
这就好像是，嗯，因为他有这样一个心结。

00:43:24.030 --> 00:43:24.727
He didn't get it.
他没有得到。

00:43:24.727 --> 00:43:25.425
He didn't get it.
他没有得到。

00:43:25.425 --> 00:43:26.410
He didn't get the story.
他没有得到那个故事。

00:43:26.410 --> 00:43:27.190
He wants the story.
他想要那个故事。

00:43:27.190 --> 00:43:28.750
So so he has to create it for himself.
所以，所以他必须为自己创造一个。

00:43:28.750 --> 00:43:30.630
He's being the parent and he didn't have.
他正在扮演他曾经没有的那个父母。

00:43:30.630 --> 00:43:33.030
We see them make a little progress in this episode.
我们看到他们在这一集中取得了一点进展。

00:43:33.030 --> 00:43:37.429
Ted learns about his parents in a way he didn't know before, but it's only a little progress.
泰德以一种他以前不知道的方式了解了他的父母，但这只是一小步进展。

00:43:37.429 --> 00:43:39.389
You don't know if it might revert the next week.
你不知道下周它会不会又倒退回去。

00:43:39.389 --> 00:43:46.230
In fact, the tag reverts them back to, I'm gonna get some juice instead of telling you your grandmother died.
事实上，片尾彩蛋又把他们打回了原形，变成“我去买点果汁”而不是“我要告诉你你奶奶去世了”。

00:43:46.230 --> 00:43:52.469
But Ted calls his shot like Babe Ruth, I'm gonna tell this story one day and by God he does it.
但泰德像贝比·鲁斯一样，提前放话了，我总有一天要讲这个故事，天呐，他真的做到了。

00:43:52.469 --> 00:43:53.469
And here we are.
然后我们就在这里了。

00:43:53.469 --> 00:44:00.670
You know how I make your mother always, even in, like this is a big capital C comedy episode.
你知道我是怎么让你们的母亲……总是这样，哪怕是在，比如这算是一集大写C的喜剧。

00:44:00.670 --> 00:44:05.969
Like this is a funny episode, a lot of actions, broken plates, like it's farce.
就像这是一集很搞笑的剧，有很多动作戏、碎盘子，简直就像闹剧一样。

00:44:05.969 --> 00:44:09.869
But I think in almost every episode, you could probably map this.
但我认为几乎在每一集里，你都可以找出这样的轨迹。

00:44:09.869 --> 00:44:13.030
There's always a moment that's just a little more wistful.
总有一个时刻会显得稍微有那么一点惆怅。

00:44:13.030 --> 00:44:15.789
Or a little more of like an exhale, you know?
或者稍微有一点像松了一口气的感觉，你知道吗？

00:44:15.789 --> 00:44:18.590
And that for me, this moment was that in this episode.
对我来说，这一集的那个时刻就是这样。

00:44:18.590 --> 00:44:24.789
Yeah, we did not feel like we were great farce writers or great, like here's one big long 12 minute funny scene.
是的，我们并不觉得自己是什么伟大的闹剧编剧，或者觉得“看，这里有一个长达12分钟的好笑场景”。

00:44:24.789 --> 00:44:26.110
It's going to be slamming doors.
那将是一场伴随着摔门声的闹剧。

00:44:26.110 --> 00:44:27.710
This is our version of that.
这就是我们对那种风格的诠释。

00:44:27.710 --> 00:44:37.349
Our version of slamming doors and breaking plates is we will jump around back and forth to the beginning and the end of the story three times and chop it up, but this is our farce.
我们拍滑稽剧的方式——也就是那种重重关上门、砸碎盘子的桥段——就是把故事的开头和结尾来来回回跳切三次，剪得支离破碎，这就是我们的滑稽闹剧。

00:44:37.349 --> 00:44:42.829
This is the way we, on how much mother, tell the farce episode and tell the slamming doors episode.
这就是我们这些“老爸老妈”剧组的人，拍摄滑稽剧和关门戏的方式。

00:44:42.829 --> 00:44:49.150
And I loved that about it because it has that energy of a zany farce episode, but it is a very pulp fiction along the way.
我特别喜欢这一点，因为它有一种疯狂滑稽剧的能量，但一路走来又非常具有低俗小说（Pulp Fiction）的风格。

00:44:49.150 --> 00:44:50.510
And I think I really liked that.
我觉得我真的很喜欢这样。

00:44:50.510 --> 00:44:54.510
I think early in season two, we thought, we have to come out with some really big comedy episodes.
我想在第二季刚开始时，我们就觉得，我们必须推出一些非常盛大的喜剧剧集。

00:44:54.510 --> 00:45:06.269
We need energy, we need to grab an audience, but I don't think we sacrificed in this one, at least what made the show rhythmically interesting and eccentric, and I like that.
我们需要活力，我们需要吸引观众，但我觉得我们在这集里并没有做出牺牲——至少没有牺牲这部剧在节奏上的趣味和古怪，我挺喜欢这一点的。

00:45:06.269 --> 00:45:08.030
So this is a fun one to rewatch.
所以重温这一集很有意思。

00:45:08.030 --> 00:45:09.989
No, it was a, it's a really fun one.
不，这集真的很搞笑、很有意思。

00:45:09.989 --> 00:45:10.909
I like this episode.
我很喜欢这一集。

00:45:10.909 --> 00:45:13.989
Stephen Lloyd, props, well done, well written.
斯蒂芬·劳埃德（Stephen Lloyd），道具组，干得漂亮，写得真好。

00:45:13.989 --> 00:45:17.340
And yeah, this one holds up.
而且，这一集经得起时间考验。

00:45:17.340 --> 00:45:18.820
A lot of the others, I don't know.
至于其他很多集，我就不知道了。

00:45:18.820 --> 00:45:24.460
I don't know.
我真不知道。

00:45:24.460 --> 00:45:34.380
We have been informed by our wonderful producer, Alec Lev, that we got a great question in this week that we'd like to spend a couple minutes answering.
我们伟大的制片人亚历克·莱夫（Alec Lev）通知我们，本周我们收到了一个非常棒的问题，我们想花几分钟时间来回答它。

00:45:34.380 --> 00:46:01.853
But also, if you want to write a question to us, if you want to share a tale or a story or anything about what How I Met Your Mother means to you when you discovered the show, how many times you watched it, why you keep watching it over and over and over again, please go to howwemadeyourmother.com, Quimium.com, go to contact and should be pretty self-explanatory, but we'd love to hear from you, whether it's an audio note or a written note.
不过另外，如果你想给我们写信提问，如果你想分享一个故事、一段经历，或者任何关于《老爸老妈浪漫史》对你意味着什么、你是什么时候发现这部剧的、你看了多少遍、为什么你一遍又一遍地反复观看它的感受，请访问 howwemadeyourmother.com 或 Quimium.com，点击“联系我们”（contact），页面说明应该很清楚了。不管是用语音留言还是文字留言，我们都非常期待听到你的声音。

00:46:01.853 --> 00:46:03.900
And Alec, what was this question?
亚历克，这个问题是什么？

00:46:04.460 --> 00:46:11.659
This is from our very good friend to the show, Swarly Memes, again, for the win with their question.
这个问题来自我们节目的忠实老朋友“Swarly Memes”，他们再次凭这个问题胜出了。

00:46:12.380 --> 00:46:19.251
Swarly says, in this episode, Ted finds out that his parents divorced after realizing how different they were.
Swarly 问，在这一集中，泰德发现他的父母在意识到彼此有多大不同后离婚了。

00:46:19.251 --> 00:46:24.935
One of those differences being that his dad always wanted kids while his his mom never did.
其中一个不同点是，他爸爸一直想要孩子，而他妈妈从来不想要。

00:46:24.935 --> 00:46:31.245
Ironically, Ted is about to have to make a similar choice with Robin, who also doesn't want children.
具有讽刺意味的是，泰德即将和同样不想要孩子的罗宾面临类似的抉择。

00:46:31.245 --> 00:46:37.929
Even though Ted didn't really know this about his parents, he still seems to be repeating the same pattern.
尽管泰德之前并不真正了解父母的这段经历，但他似乎仍在重复同样的模式。

00:46:37.929 --> 00:46:50.610
Do you think that we sometimes carry these kinds of generational patterns with us, almost like unconscious curses that can pass down whether through family dynamics or even something deeper like our DNA?
你认为我们有时会把这种代际模式带在身边吗？就像无意识的诅咒一样，通过家族动态甚至更深层的DNA代代相传？

00:46:50.610 --> 00:46:52.369
Damn, Swarly memes.
天哪，Swarly memes。

00:46:52.369 --> 00:46:53.849
It's a smart question.
这是一个很聪明的问题。

00:46:53.849 --> 00:46:56.010
It's a disturbing and smart question.
这是一个让人不安又聪明的问题。

00:46:56.010 --> 00:47:01.750
And in some ways, I'm like, I don't know if we are credentialed to answer that.
在某些方面，我觉得，我不知道我们是否有资格回答这个问题。

