He was in Utah.
他在犹他州。

00:56:10.630 --> 00:56:11.645
Yeah, it was.
对，是这样的。

00:56:11.645 --> 00:56:14.846
We moved that out of journey, that story.
我们把这段经历从旅程中移除了。

00:56:14.846 --> 00:56:15.346
Yeah.
对。

00:56:15.346 --> 00:56:19.719
You know, I love that your teacher showed you the pilot.
你知道的，我很喜欢你的老师给你们看试播集。

00:56:19.610 --> 00:56:20.989
It's mind-blowing.
这太不可思议了。

00:56:20.989 --> 00:56:32.670
I wonder what, I wonder in what context, but I remember I had this English teacher, my freshman year of high school named Mrs. LaMuth, and I had her also my senior year of creative writing.
我想知道，在什么背景下，但我记得我上高一的时候有位英语老师叫拉穆斯太太，高三上创意写作课时也是她教我。

00:56:32.670 --> 00:56:35.070
Great teacher, she passed away a couple years ago.
一位伟大的老师，她几年前去世了。

00:56:35.070 --> 00:56:41.750
And before she died, she dropped off all my creative writing that I did for her as a senior, she had saved it.
在她去世前，她把我高三时为她写的所有创意写作作业都留了下来，她一直保存着。

00:56:41.750 --> 00:56:42.250
Wow.
哦，哇。

00:56:42.250 --> 00:56:46.633
She just left it for as an envelope on our, my mom said Mrs. LaMuth dropped this.
她只是把它们作为一个信封留在我们家，我妈妈说拉穆斯太太送来了这个。

00:56:46.349 --> 00:56:49.349
So I got to see all this writing I had done as a senior in high school.
所以我得以看到我高中最后一年写的所有这些文章。

00:56:49.349 --> 00:56:55.949
But I remember one day she brought a turntable in, and she played Carol King's tapestry, the song tapestry.
但我记得有一天她带了一个唱盘进来，放了卡罗尔·金的《挂毯》，那首叫《挂毯》的歌。

00:56:55.949 --> 00:56:56.789
Yeah, yeah, yeah.
对，对，对。

00:56:56.789 --> 00:56:59.550
And she gave us the lyrics and we analyzed it like poetry.
她把歌词发给我们，我们像分析诗歌一样分析它。

00:56:59.550 --> 00:57:00.829
She just wanted us to take it through.
她只是想让我们仔细品味。

00:57:00.829 --> 00:57:05.869
And from that moment on, like I've been a huge Carol King fan, that album isn't masterful.
从那一刻起，我就成了卡罗尔·金的超级粉丝，那张专辑简直是大师级的作品。

00:57:05.869 --> 00:57:07.750
I'm a masterwork, yeah.
是一部杰作，对。

00:57:07.750 --> 00:57:11.829
But that song in particular is so lyrically dense and rich and so beautiful.
但尤其是那首歌，歌词意境深厚、丰富又极其美丽。

00:57:11.829 --> 00:57:21.071
And it really, now that I write songs, I even have a deeper appreciation for it, but it really taught me how songs could be poetry and artful.
现在我自己也写歌了，我对它有了更深的欣赏，但它确实教会了我歌曲如何可以像诗歌一样充满艺术感。

00:57:21.071 --> 00:57:29.531
It was such a great lesson, but I'm just struck by the power that teachers have to introduce students to art and things they love.
那是一堂非常棒的课，但我深深地感受到老师们引导学生接触艺术和他们所爱之物的巨大力量。

00:57:29.531 --> 00:57:32.590
It can affect them for the rest of their lives.
这会影响他们的一生。

00:57:32.590 --> 00:57:33.965
Yeah, it's phenomenal.
对，这太了不起了。

00:57:33.965 --> 00:57:40.155
It's such an honor when we hear how much mother in a classroom is just so mind-blowing and so cool.
当我们听到有人在课堂上提到《老妈》时，真的是让人脑洞大开又酷毙了。

00:57:40.155 --> 00:57:42.407
Thank you, Jonathan, for that story.
谢谢你，乔纳森，分享这个故事。

00:57:42.407 --> 00:57:43.469
It's really cool.
真的很酷。

00:57:43.469 --> 00:57:49.106
Also, it might not look like it in the moment, but things might be organizing themselves on your behalf.
还有，当时可能看起来不像，但事情可能正在为你默默安排。

00:57:49.106 --> 00:57:50.623
I think I've mentioned this.
我想我提到过这个。

00:57:50.623 --> 00:57:53.821
I think it's either Larry McMurtry or Cormac McCarthy line.
我觉得这是麦卡锡或科马克·麦卡锡的名言。

00:57:53.821 --> 00:57:57.398
You never know what worst luck you're bad luck is saving you from.
你永远不知道最糟糕的运气正在帮你免受怎样的厄运。

00:57:57.398 --> 00:57:58.807
Yeah, that's a great line.
是的，这是一句名言。

00:57:58.807 --> 00:58:09.429
You know it looks like it looks like things are you know but it's all coalescing and it's I think I think how our mother says look it's a it's a friendly universe it's on your side and just trust.
你知道的，看起来事情好像是这样，但它们都在汇聚，而且我认为，正如《老妈》所说，看吧，这是一个友善的宇宙，它站在你这边，去相信它就行了。

